# Shell scripts keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# Without this, a checkout on Windows rewrites them to CRLF, and the \r rides
# along into the interpreter: bash reads `set -o pipefail\r`, calls it an
# invalid option and stops on line 13. The script is fine; the file is not.
# Cheap to prevent, and confusing to diagnose the first time it happens --
# nothing in the error mentions line endings.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by tools that do not expect a carriage return.
*.py text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Binaries git must not touch.
*.so binary
*.png binary
*.db binary
*.mp4 binary

# Kept out of the language bar, not out of the repository.
#
# GitHub reads the language bar off every tracked file, so ten Solidity files
# make a Solana project advertise Solidity on its front page. Nine of them are
# the archived EVM contracts in `legacy/evm/` -- they compile, the backend
# never called them, and they were never deployed to a public network, which
# section 11 of the whitepaper says in as many words. The tenth is a vendored
# OpenZeppelin interface they import.
#
# `linguist-vendored` removes them from the statistics only. The directories
# stay listed, browsable, and explained by their own README. Hiding them would
# be the opposite of what this repository is for; letting them describe the
# project is just as wrong.
legacy/** linguist-vendored
.deps/** linguist-vendored
